Approach 3 — Your Code ⭐

Your approach scans right → left.

The stack maintains possible discount prices.

For the current price:

while (!st.isEmpty() && st.peek() > prices[i]) {
    st.pop();
}

After removing prices greater than the current price:

stack.peek() is the first smaller-or-equal price to the right.
If stack is empty → no discount.

Your Code
class Solution {
    public int[] finalPrices(int[] prices) {
        
        int n=prices.length;
        int ans[]=new int[n];
        Stack<Integer> st=new Stack<>();
        
        for(int i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
            
            while(!st.isEmpty()&&st.peek()>prices[i]){
                st.pop();
            }

            if(!st.isEmpty()){
                ans[i]=prices[i]-st.peek();
            } else {
                ans[i]=prices[i];
            }
            
            st.push(prices[i]);
        }
        
        return ans;
    }
}

This is an optimal O(n) solution.

Why Does Your Stack Work?

Suppose:

prices = [8, 4, 6, 2, 3]

When processing 6, the elements to its right are:

2, 3

We need the first price <= 6.

That's:

2

The stack helps us remove elements that cannot be the answer.

The rule:

st.peek() > prices[i]

means:

If the stack top is greater than the current price, it can never be the discount for the current price.

So remove it.

